ive -20; next period it is -25. The cash release is +5. Reversing the balance to +20 then +25 and reversing the movement calculation can still yield +5. Check the balance definition and the movement independently.\n\n### Example: displayed units can already convert\n\nA stored value of 2,400 in thousands may display as 2.4 million because of its number format. Dividing by 1,000 in the formula and retaining that format converts twice. Check raw value, format and display together. Similarly, do not combine a current-year numerator with a future-year denominator without support from the task or model.\n\n## R3. Keep checks independent; reconcile opening bases\n\nFor each claimed validation, ask: “What possible mistake would make this fail?” If the answer is none because of how you defined the inputs, it is a consistency identity, not independent validation.\n\n### Example: a plug destroys its own detector\n\nAssets are 150, modeled liabilities are 95, and independently supported equity is 50. The balance difference is 5. Setting equity to `150-95=55` forces zero but hides the mismatch. Derive equity from its supported opening balance and movements, then diagnose the remaining 5.\n\nAn explicitly requested residual is legitimate. In that case, do not use the resulting zero as evidence that the other components are correct. Do not invent opening cash, equity or retained earnings merely to obtain a neat reconciliation.\n\n### Example: incompatible opening debt bases\n\nThe historical balance sheet closes debt at 80. A financing table starts at 86. If forecast movement is -5, simply selecting 86-5 instead of 80-5 introduces a persistent 6 difference. Determine whether the table includes a documented transaction, different scope or adjustment; reconcile that difference. Do not silently select a convenient opening base or offset the discrepancy in equity.\n\nA rule about a charge is not automatically a rule about the underlying stock: assigning a noncash interest charge to a